WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:03.470
Let's see, let's go next two.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.700
Why don't we go to our caller from the two
oh three area code.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.309
Who's calling today from two oh three.

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:10.860
Hey David, this is me.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.200
Yes, that's you.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.200
Yeah.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.200
Hi.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.820
Um, so I'm curious about your take about the
supreme court.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:24.560
Um, I know recently brain serious came out
and said that they, he's against, um, increasing

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:31.300
the number of judges in Supreme Court and,
but he's pro, um, I guess circuit of like

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.640
judges come through.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.230
Uh, what was your take on that?

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.370
Yeah, I, I, uh, I've seen some convincing
arguments about stacking the court, packing

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.950
the court and increasing the number of justices.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:47.410
I do also think that it's one of those things
where if if we do it then it's opening up

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:53.780
the door for them to do it and the, the reality
is that right now the left has much more to

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.149
lose from not changing the, the structure
of the Supreme Court because Donald Trump

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:02.700
partially thanks to Mitch McConnell's obstruction
of Merrick garland has already had two supreme

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.200
court picks and there is potentially going
to be a very conservative supreme court for

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.200
a long time.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.180
So I understand the appeal although I worry
about opening up that door.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.970
Um, in terms of the idea of supreme court
justices not serving for life, I, I find that

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.320
very interesting as well.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.280
The only problem is like with all of these
things, once you know you are not going to

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:31.880
be serving for life, you potentially could
be influenced in terms of how you decide cases

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.600
based on whether maybe there is a party to
one of those cases that after you are no longer

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.760
on the court you might consult for or work
for or whatever.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.990
And it creates a lot of complicated issues
related to conflicts of interest and recusal.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.960
So I like a lot of these ideas and I also
recognize some of the shortfalls of them.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:51.960
Okay.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:02:01.660
Yeah, I've, uh, seen, uh, some, some proposal
builds of like, uh, I mean, uh, judges and,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:10.720
uh, both a congressman and the Senate, uh,
to, you know, like not get into the industry

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.660
is after they leave office.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.660
Um,

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.860
well that's the thing, if you limit with,
with Supreme Court justices, if you say you

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:23.230
no longer have a lifetime appointment, but
you also have to agree not to work for any

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:27.360
of the industries whose cases you hear while
you're on the court, it becomes almost impossible,

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.360
right?

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.360
Like what are they expected to do?

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.590
Because Supreme Court hears so many different
cases.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.820
So I don't know what the solution is, but
I think that we, there's definitely improvements

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.820
to be made.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:36.820
All right.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:37.950
Thank you David.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:38.950
Okay.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:39.950
Appreciate the phone call.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:39.954
Thank you very much.

